  faqs={[
    {q:"Can I live in Coronado without a car?", a:"Practically, no. While Coronado Centro has some walkable areas around the main strip, getting to supermarkets, the beach clubs, medical clinics, or neighboring towns like San Carlos requires a vehicle. Taxis exist but are unreliable late at night. A car (or at minimum a scooter) is essential for comfortable daily life here."},
    {q:"How is internet connectivity in Coronado?", a:"Internet in Coronado has improved significantly in recent years. Cable & Wireless and Claro both offer cable broadband in the main residential areas, with speeds typically 30–100 Mbps. Fiber is not universally available outside the core zones. Remote workers should verify coverage for their specific address before signing a lease — speeds in rural pockets or Santa Clara can still be inconsistent."},
    {q:"Is Coronado a year-round destination or mainly seasonal?", a:"Both. Coronado is fully inhabited year-round, but sees a noticeable seasonal shift. The dry season (January–April) is peak beach weather — sunny, breezy, and busy. The rainy season (May–November) brings daily afternoon showers and greener landscapes. Many Panama City residents use Coronado as a weekend home, so restaurants and shops are busier on weekends. Full-time expat residents enjoy quieter weekdays year-round."},
    {q:"What visa do retirees in Coronado typically use?", a:"The Pensionado visa is the most common choice — requiring at least $1,000/month in guaranteed pension income, it grants permanent residency and significant discounts (20–50%) on medical services, airfares, hotels, and entertainment. For retirees without a pension but with savings, the Friendly Nations visa or the Self-Economic Solvency visa (demonstrating a minimum monthly income of $2,000) are solid alternatives."},
    {q:"Are there international schools near Coronado?", a:"No — Coronado has local Panamanian schools but no international schools. The nearest IB or American-curriculum schools are in Panama City (about 80km away). Most expat families with school-age children who need international education commute to the capital or live full-time in Panama City and use Coronado as a weekend or holiday retreat."},
  ]}
